#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221119 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "5399454b-0691-5cb3-884f-26af4ca52f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-21671" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - y, \\qquad y(1) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈0.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221120 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "62a1e90b-05a5-5828-abe0-fa5cbb4c1c1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21672" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x^2 - 2y, \\qquad y(3) = -1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 4.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value $x_\\text{new}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}-2y+(3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221121 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/id #uuid "d387bb78-9df1-5551-9e1e-5a4745c5b731" :question/math-academy-id "e-21673" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - y, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-0}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4x-y)+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+1.33) \\\\  & ≈4.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33,\n\\qquad\ny(2) \\approx 4.44.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5f7dc3-d1b6-5ae2-a5c9-1080a9243167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2y}{2-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327144} {:answer/id #uuid "08beff13-be0c-52a3-bf5f-1c05861e586c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y}{2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327145} {:answer/id #uuid "6041104d-c2b9-5cfb-810e-5b655a7ab2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327146} {:answer/id #uuid "8681ab2c-960c-5418-b300-2c55a0928b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy-2y}{2-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327147} {:answer/id #uuid "fb53397f-b30d-52ad-b82d-011a1f60304d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+y}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08beff13-be0c-52a3-bf5f-1c05861e586c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y}{2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327145} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83a6f5d-b5ab-5c2f-bb69-05a7d2c399f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6f655de-63db-57ed-a4b5-8a5ccc3c3a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-333386" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}-3xy,\\,y(-2)=3\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-3xy+({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y}{2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3651baa2-aa86-509e-b324-bff633a1c93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327151} {:answer/id #uuid "82575476-51d2-5e50-954e-fd5ab0a2de2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327152} {:answer/id #uuid "fb722a6e-633d-556e-8316-ad260d2e8ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327153} {:answer/id #uuid "8c80717f-6d7b-5ff4-8d17-bc77bd30f58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327154} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6722aa-cba0-561e-a2c3-9fdf3bb0a377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa6722aa-cba0-561e-a2c3-9fdf3bb0a377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327155} :answer-field/id #uuid "248a4b19-7988-5e3d-8f64-19186d4b7b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efc31e39-7aa7-5d03-bc87-5224d0577657" :question/math-academy-id "q-334595" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+3y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x+3y+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b54c24f-d8c7-57aa-90ce-83158007c0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327158} {:answer/id #uuid "23c99a28-53ae-5e21-afa2-0b1acf82f3f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-22y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327159} {:answer/id #uuid "f13d41e2-b0dc-5cd4-b4e0-80b65cf651c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-21y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327160} {:answer/id #uuid "e97c78fd-b193-524e-a260-3620d03ed871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(19x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327161} {:answer/id #uuid "b26723e7-2379-5867-ba98-a2ebf7007ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+19{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b54c24f-d8c7-57aa-90ce-83158007c0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327158} :answer-field/id #uuid "7930f171-bef9-5bf5-88e4-7f761f34b871" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47106ae6-210a-505e-8456-455c5fb7e23e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334596" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-5y,\\,y(-2)=1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=5.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-5y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{27}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e94dd726-000c-5e23-b2b8-17324c3babb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{2-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327165} {:answer/id #uuid "6b69380a-9cd5-566f-ad5c-1bca8628d1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327166} {:answer/id #uuid "f44b268d-9cad-5592-b220-10dcf2c10f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327167} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbefe1b-0540-5578-bdf7-d1884d2228ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+(x+1)y}{1-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327168} {:answer/id #uuid "dd690be8-e6ef-5935-b6e6-6eade88f5e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}^{2}y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b69380a-9cd5-566f-ad5c-1bca8628d1ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327166} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3f1893-d9b4-5f98-9440-1bd3e94ae2b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec6aaba4-a9af-5820-a3d2-81b896a43353" :question/math-academy-id "q-334602" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=2.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}y+({e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}^{2}y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}^{2}y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb4cff8-7336-562d-87e3-557736c82ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-4xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327172} {:answer/id #uuid "5be0154d-1d87-5e90-9934-1879e7bca74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{1+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327173} {:answer/id #uuid "de0bb658-12cf-5286-80c8-98a4f3a4bc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327174} {:answer/id #uuid "a31343db-d7ca-52c8-b64e-c35269a34aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327175} {:answer/id #uuid "71249c32-591e-587c-bff4-8ea5bd92b8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de0bb658-12cf-5286-80c8-98a4f3a4bc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327174} :answer-field/id #uuid "07aa40d3-3ed7-5db2-8a50-7a6af020d840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf464e43-7201-5bd2-b696-bb306f276b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334603" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=3.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdd02c9-756f-5641-8900-9d6178d3fad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 7.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220655} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe6f2db-ca7b-5538-bda2-79e4604ba76c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220656} {:answer/id #uuid "502eaf14-8a1a-5666-9a19-e5c14d9b39e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 6.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220657} {:answer/id #uuid "65e89271-23f1-5109-bb51-24788b9cc527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd02c9-756f-5641-8900-9d6178d3fad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 7.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220655} :answer-field/id #uuid "08733dc5-d9bd-53d6-a342-1c132cf6db66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58e58a09-2ef4-5a43-9680-2188ba55e132" :question/math-academy-id "q-339628" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & ≈-7.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-7.77.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefa4c10-fbdb-5e61-a219-eb8f9d044ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327179} {:answer/id #uuid "1ceb1714-b031-513b-adf0-0dbfb3944a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327180} {:answer/id #uuid "a99a6195-cafa-5bc9-9c6c-6d7d97edfbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327181} {:answer/id #uuid "00879e08-d91e-5326-b045-9f72eb3d109b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00879e08-d91e-5326-b045-9f72eb3d109b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327182} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57e8378-72fd-50ff-a4fc-73bc2b4fa48e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c612c4-c022-50fa-be61-aceb89da0322" :question/math-academy-id "q-339748" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(3),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x+4y+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+4{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y+4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+3-5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1.33.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bd22b0-ddd7-57e4-99fa-d60df624b9ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327185} {:answer/id #uuid "29706f05-1354-5c15-8ae1-feb185d03c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327186} {:answer/id #uuid "8f794ead-d434-5aeb-91ad-61747ee201e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327187} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d8e728-276c-58f4-955b-b1e334804b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10bd22b0-ddd7-57e4-99fa-d60df624b9ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327185} :answer-field/id #uuid "71871eec-880a-5515-a543-0013f56fed4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6da7d666-79b5-54a3-bca8-7b06a3e3d9e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339758" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-3y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{3}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(-\\frac{1}{3})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{3}-(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-3y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x-3y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{4}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-1+(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & ≈-0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{3})≈-0.89.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc712bdd-e38b-5f53-9317-2b58044aac26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327191} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6b39e0-663a-5323-9b02-18d2bffd50c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327192} {:answer/id #uuid "535a0bca-ea96-5e5d-b446-fdde75261864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327193} {:answer/id #uuid "ed112d5e-f09b-5311-92f6-b886c884f4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed112d5e-f09b-5311-92f6-b886c884f4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327194} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fe9b543-53d4-58f8-8988-b7eca00c99b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "458dd29e-fddf-5836-a3d7-f0a5c19bb254" :question/math-academy-id "q-339764" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=\\frac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y({x}^{2}+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{3}((-1)^{2}+5)}{3-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{(\\frac{11}{4})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})≈8.00.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b824e49d-3aaa-55a8-b139-e8618b4e1d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220686} {:answer/id #uuid "05be6451-b36e-58a8-8a17-6159cf75d7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220687} {:answer/id #uuid "6976b06e-1190-5bd7-afd7-b22da85e7159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b824e49d-3aaa-55a8-b139-e8618b4e1d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220686} :answer-field/id #uuid "779a6c9d-6a6c-5175-bb5e-bbacad42a1a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fb805c7-d341-5037-96c5-7f740432fea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220690} {:answer/id #uuid "66e18b68-0555-5931-8e9c-80b0c83aa03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220691} {:answer/id #uuid "71f60511-9f7d-5c0f-937e-fb877cba3b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 9.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e18b68-0555-5931-8e9c-80b0c83aa03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220691} :answer-field/id #uuid "975dd5cd-2028-5691-a4d1-07765228d5d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05d2dcb4-6d58-5ba1-932c-6420aacb3731" :question/math-academy-id "q-339771" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+4y,\\,y(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(0)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x+4y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 0+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =-10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈3.00,\\,y(1)≈-10.50.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8089bce0-7d9d-51db-8964-fb4c14564846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327197} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc187b3-8f94-5bb0-94d3-9d961078fc6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327198} {:answer/id #uuid "a24b03e4-5d46-5171-a0a1-701ae18d1224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc187b3-8f94-5bb0-94d3-9d961078fc6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327198} :answer-field/id #uuid "87835a36-3966-5c12-97b9-f95d14dc6002" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213149e7-2f19-5563-938e-e85ab5bdd858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327201} {:answer/id #uuid "57d2c519-b7ba-58d8-a8f5-199f7f3efe76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327202} {:answer/id #uuid "056eab04-5053-5869-ad91-eed8c9caa441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056eab04-5053-5869-ad91-eed8c9caa441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327203} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c216f0-1cb5-5275-8b6b-9178d5defd8d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19c96779-c454-5d47-8125-893e8be80f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339856" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=0.20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2-0.20) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\  & =1.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0.20,\\,y(2)≈1.16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3440780b-e39d-50dc-ad6c-552ae9d4490c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327206} {:answer/id #uuid "9726ad27-aefb-5b9f-8792-6e0978fe521a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327207} {:answer/id #uuid "20949bf0-84c0-5987-8c09-d494adc2012b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20949bf0-84c0-5987-8c09-d494adc2012b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327208} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3117ce-e931-5e73-b7ba-2a07e3e9a6e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327205} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875ff9ff-da85-5d5b-8d6a-9295697ac74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327210} {:answer/id #uuid "1485026f-5290-507f-b793-2871337927f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327211} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe64ba0-583c-5968-a043-ce7161062a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1485026f-5290-507f-b793-2871337927f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327211} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2efb0f-4c39-583a-bd64-db0a06015b23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06e11280-02d9-55eb-ba08-45ad211f8038" :question/math-academy-id "q-339859" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+5y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(0)$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+5y)+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+7y)+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+7y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-1+0-7\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-7y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+1-7\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈-5.00,\\,y(1)≈12.00.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20454901-3735-5f9d-955d-871f04ed77c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327215} {:answer/id #uuid "665ef483-d00d-5a42-a4ba-b9e3741988e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327216} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8320fc-e10e-5e15-9c5e-b84c8ec8ef58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20454901-3735-5f9d-955d-871f04ed77c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327215} :answer-field/id #uuid "6103fb35-3b73-5929-897b-69890440ecbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3967edab-82b4-5920-bacb-0ad4f99dc58d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-111.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327219} {:answer/id #uuid "d409b956-02ce-58e5-a037-5143057dec53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-124.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327220} {:answer/id #uuid "8611ba05-0073-5655-a50a-d5baadd18cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-122.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d409b956-02ce-58e5-a037-5143057dec53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-124.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327220} :answer-field/id #uuid "18fea86a-b3c6-512b-ac9e-9331911216b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37b67cda-0790-56a0-a9b2-3aa62264a960" :question/math-academy-id "q-339868" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+5y,\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+5y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+9\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =-124.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈13.00,\\,y(2)≈-124.00.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9923254-46db-5e7e-a783-29f6bdb9e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327224} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba320a3-ee4b-5f6e-9393-dae3962b5cc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327225} {:answer/id #uuid "75cabf06-344c-5a77-b79b-ccdf0351b8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75cabf06-344c-5a77-b79b-ccdf0351b8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327226} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a75f88-1730-549e-82a7-e65fb82e77a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327223} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d085a196-35f0-5a9d-a5f7-ad433679375c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327228} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0c09f5-94c6-592b-8174-6b46b1b4e115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327229} {:answer/id #uuid "f08ef5e5-4b94-5036-a76a-fa2b1840e88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0c09f5-94c6-592b-8174-6b46b1b4e115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab68f67-1e55-5d1a-93e5-69b03d50c499" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75d16960-c949-5e39-a9af-ee95bd4a9ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-339869" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-3x+y)+(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4},\\,y=3.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+9\\cdot (3.75)) \\\\  & =4.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈3.75,\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.50.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67a7e066-0635-5901-bd89-0bcc0aa50625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327233} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87955e-4e64-58af-a204-a1dc557be326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327234} {:answer/id #uuid "b06d13b9-58a7-5a7f-b822-eeb0e0d1658c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327235} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae6fc48-84d7-5fd1-b8c2-4a5667bbfcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c87955e-4e64-58af-a204-a1dc557be326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327234} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af19939-d3a7-5351-bada-8e1bd7f240d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d6facfa-0fa0-5956-91e6-6ceec0b05639" :question/math-academy-id "q-339873" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(3\\sqrt{x}+2),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(3\\sqrt{0}+4)}{3\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈-2.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-2.67.$"}]}
